  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Michigan saw $5.4B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. Award activity reached 152,303 awards, with an average award size of $35.8K. Obligations fell 17.5% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $2.7B in Michigan, and the General Services Administration obligated $1.9B. Automobile Manufacturing is the largest industry by obligations at $1.8B, followed by Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing at $1.3B. GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. is the top vendor with $1.3B in obligations, and FORD MOTOR CO follows with $678M. Historical obligations show FY21 at $6.8B, FY22 at $9B, FY23 at $8.4B, FY24 at $8B, FY25 at $6.6B, and FY26 at $5.4B. The top ten buying agencies include the Department of Veterans Affairs at $211M, the Department of Homeland Security at $205M, and other agencies that range down to the Department of Housing and Urban Development at $28.2M. The top industries beyond the leaders include Other Engine Equipment Manufacturing at $149M and Petroleum Refineries at $143M.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in Michigan?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $2.7B in obligations, and the General Services Administration is the second largest with $1.9B. The top vendor, GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC., accounts for $1.3B while the largest industry, Automobile Manufacturing, accounts for $1.8B. Total obligations for FY26 to date are $5.4B, with 152,303 awards and an average award size of $35.8K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How has Michigan’s federal contracting trended recently?",
        "body": "Obligations in FY26 to date are $5.4B, down 17.5% year over year. Historical obligations were $6.8B in FY21, $9B in FY22, $8.4B in FY23, $8B in FY24, and $6.6B in FY25. The year-to-date totals are partial for the fiscal year."
      }
